{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "01 — Meet Bolovan",
  "description": "Begin outside the implementation and move inward. You will meet the product promise, learn the repository's language, see why the architecture exists, and finish with a map of the runtime modules. The next chapter follows one real request through that map.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "nextTour": "02 — Follow One Request",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^# Bolovan$",
      "title": "01.01 — Start with the user's promise",
      "description": "Imagine opening Obsidian on a phone or desktop and finding an agent in the sidebar. The README is that user's view: one OpenAI-compatible endpoint, bounded access to the vault, visible tool calls, resumable conversations, and explicit approval before changes. Keep this promise in mind; every module you meet exists to preserve some part of it."
    },
    {
      "file": "manifest.json",
      "pattern": "\"id\": \"bolovan\"",
      "title": "01.02 — Obsidian sees one ordinary plugin",
      "description": "Before any TypeScript runs, this manifest tells Obsidian what Bolovan is. The minimum app version is 1.13.4 and `isDesktopOnly` is false, so the same plugin must remain truthful on desktop, iOS, and Android. That small flag rules out native processes and Node-only runtime shortcuts."
    },
    {
      "file": "CONTEXT.md",
      "pattern": "^# Bolovan — domain model$",
      "title": "01.03 — Learn the cast of characters",
      "description": "The story becomes easier once its nouns stop blurring together. A **Run** is one user-triggered execution; the **Harness** conducts it; a **Prepared change** waits for approval; the **Brain** stores portable knowledge; a **Conversation** can have device-owned **Branches**; and the **Transcript** is what the user sees. Read the glossary now so those names carry one meaning later."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/adr/0001-native-cross-platform-harness.md",
      "pattern": "^## Context$",
      "title": "01.04 — See the problem that shaped the system",
      "description": "Bolovan cannot ask users to install a sidecar process, and a Node-oriented agent SDK would break the mobile promise. Obsidian already supplies portable boundaries for files, network requests, and secrets. The architecture begins by accepting those constraints instead of hiding them."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/adr/0001-native-cross-platform-harness.md",
      "pattern": "^## Decision$",
      "title": "01.05 — Choose two deep seams",
      "description": "The accepted decision is deliberately small: Bolovan owns its Harness, speaks to one normalized model adapter, and exposes one bounded model-facing tool interface backed by Obsidian APIs. Everything else should deepen those seams before inventing another one."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/main.ts",
      "pattern": "async onload\\(\\): Promise<void>",
      "title": "01.06 — Enter through the composition root",
      "description": "Now the source begins. `BolovanPlugin.onload` restores device-local settings, creates a stable device id when necessary, constructs the single long-lived `BolovanAgent`, and registers the chat view, commands, settings, and workspace event. This method connects the pieces; it does not own the agent's run policy."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/bolovan-agent.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class BolovanAgent",
      "title": "01.07 — Meet the conductor",
      "description": "`BolovanAgent` is the Harness. It owns single-flight Runs, provider calls, tool rounds, approvals, cancellation, and conversation persistence behind one interface used by the view. The next tour will stay here long enough to watch those responsibilities unfold in order."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/model-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class ModelTools",
      "title": "01.08 — Meet the capability gate",
      "description": "The Harness does not hand the model an Obsidian `App`. `ModelTools` assembles a small advertised tool set and turns every invocation into either a direct result or a `PreparedChange`. Vault, workspace, and web behavior remain behind this gate."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/brain-store.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class BrainStore",
      "title": "01.09 — Meet portable memory",
      "description": "`BrainStore` owns the visible Brain folder, root `AGENTS.md`, conversations, and device-owned branches while delegating canonical package validation to the Skill module. Secrets and endpoint configuration stay outside it in device-local plugin settings. This split lets useful knowledge sync without turning credentials or approval state into shared vault data."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/chat-view.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class BolovanChatView",
      "title": "01.10 — Return to the user's sidebar",
      "description": "The chat view is the other end of the path. It renders a `Transcript`, collects messages, keeps approval cards in the conversation, and manages DOM concerns such as scrolling and session controls. It observes the Harness rather than reimplementing its policy."
    },
    {
      "file": "package.json",
      "pattern": "\"test\": \"npm run build",
      "title": "01.11 — Learn what counts as proof",
      "description": "The repository's full check is one story too: typecheck, build the production bundle, run Vitest, then load the built plugin with an Obsidian stub. `main.js` is the generated ending of that build; the authored runtime lives under `src/`. With the map established, continue to chapter 02 and follow one request through it."
    }
  ]
}